resource "aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration" "backups" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.main.id

  rule {
    id     = "backups-lifecycle"
    status = "Enabled"

    filter {
      prefix = "backups/"
    }

    transition {
      days          = 30
      storage_class = "STANDARD_IA"
    }

    transition {
      days          = 90
      storage_class = "GLACIER"
    }

    expiration {
      days = 365
    }

    noncurrent_version_expiration {
      noncurrent_days = 60
    }
  }
}
```

`filter { prefix = "backups/" }` limits the rule's scope to objects under this prefix only, not touching the rest of the bucket. The two `transition` blocks handle requirements (1) and (2): `days = 30` moves to `STANDARD_IA`, `days = 90` moves further to `GLACIER` (Glacier Flexible Retrieval) — AWS applies these in the correct increasing day order automatically, no need for manual sequential transitions. `expiration { days = 365 }` handles requirement (3) — fully deleting the CURRENT version of the object 365 days after creation. The trickiest part is requirement (4): since the bucket has versioning enabled, every new backup overwriting the object (same key) creates a NEW version and turns the previous one into "noncurrent" (no longer the latest) — but that old version is NOT automatically deleted, it still exists and consumes storage. The `expiration`/`transition` fields only apply to the CURRENT version, so they don't handle cleaning up old versions. A SEPARATE field is needed: `noncurrent_version_expiration { noncurrent_days = 60 }` — meaning exactly "60 days after this version BECAME noncurrent (no longer the latest)", not 60 days after the object was created. This is the parallel pair a versioned bucket must always consider: `transition`/`expiration` handle the current version, `noncurrent_version_transition`/`noncurrent_version_expiration` handle old versions — omitting the second pair is a common reason versioned buckets silently balloon in storage over time.

