Two main issues. **Issue 1 — missing IRSA wiring, most severe:** the code correctly creates `aws_iam_role.alb_controller` and attaches a policy to it, but there's NO `kubernetes_service_account` resource to set the `eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn` annotation pointing at that role, and `helm_release` doesn't have `set { name = "serviceAccount.create" value = "false" }` either. Consequence: the chart falls back to the default `serviceAccount.create = true`, so Helm auto-creates a brand-new `aws-load-balancer-controller` ServiceAccount with NO IRSA annotation at all — meanwhile the IAM role, though created correctly, is **never wired** to the actual running ServiceAccount. The controller Pod, running under this permission-less ServiceAccount, can't call the ELBv2 API (`CreateLoadBalancer`, `RegisterTargets`...), so EVERY Ingress/LoadBalancer-type Service in the cluster hangs forever without ever producing a real LB — a bug that's easy to miss because at a glance the IAM setup "looks correct" (both role and policy exist), but the two halves were never actually joined. Fix: add
```hcl
resource "kubernetes_service_account" "alb_controller" {
  metadata {
    name      = "aws-load-balancer-controller"
    namespace = "kube-system"
    annotations = {
      "eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn" = aws_iam_role.alb_controller.arn
    }
  }
}
```
and add `set { name = "serviceAccount.create" value = "false" }` in `helm_release` so the chart reuses the annotated ServiceAccount instead of creating a fresh one. **Issue 2 — no controlled teardown order:** there's nothing guaranteeing that Ingress/LoadBalancer-type Services get deleted BEFORE `helm_release` (the controller) gets removed by `terraform destroy`. If the controller is torn down first, any ALB/NLB it created become **orphaned resources** — no controller is watching them anymore to clean them up, and no Terraform state directly manages them either (since they were created by the controller's own API calls, not as Terraform resources) — the result is indefinite billing until someone notices and manually deletes them via the Console. Fix: bake into the operational runbook a mandatory `kubectl delete svc,ingress --all` (or scoped to the relevant namespace) and wait for the controller to actually remove the real LBs **before** running `terraform destroy` on `helm_release`; if this needs enforcing in code, consider a manual verification step or a wrapper script around `terraform destroy` rather than relying purely on someone remembering the right order.

