```typescript
append(roomId: string, userId: string, text: string): ChatMessage {
    const nextSeq = (this.counters.get(roomId) ?? 0) + 1
    this.counters.set(roomId, nextSeq)
    const msg: ChatMessage = { seq: nextSeq, userId, text, timestamp: Date.now() }
    const arr = this.history.get(roomId) ?? []
    arr.push(msg)
    if (arr.length > this.ringCap) {
        arr.shift() // evict the OLDEST message, not the newest
    }
    this.history.set(roomId, arr)
    return msg
}

replaySince(roomId: string, lastSeq: number, cap: number): ChatMessage[] {
    const arr = this.history.get(roomId) ?? []
    return arr.filter((m) => m.seq > lastSeq).slice(0, cap)
}
```
`append` issues `nextSeq` by reading the current counter for `roomId` (defaulting to 0) and adding 1 — this is the single source that increments the seq, guaranteeing MONOTONIC growth within one process, since every call builds on the previously saved value rather than generating independently. After `push`, it checks `arr.length > this.ringCap` and `shift()`s — note that `shift()` removes the FIRST element (the oldest message, since new ones are always pushed at the end), turning `history` into a memory-bounded ring buffer. `replaySince` is the heart of at-least-once delivery: the filter condition must be `m.seq > lastSeq` — strict `>`, not `>=` — because `lastSeq` is precisely the seq of the LAST message the client already confirmed seeing; using `>=` would resend the message with `seq === lastSeq` even though the client already has it, causing a visible duplicate (requiring extra dedup work on the frontend). `.slice(0, cap)` is applied right after `filter` to bound a single ack, independent from the `append` cap — one cap prevents the log from growing over time, the other prevents the payload from ballooning within one request. On seq atomicity: this exercise uses an in-memory `Map<string, number>`, which is safe for a single process because Node.js processes each handler call synchronously in sequence (no race condition between reading-then-writing the counter within the same event loop tick). But if the system scales horizontally with multiple instances writing to the same room, a per-instance in-memory counter becomes WRONG — two instances could both issue `seq = 5` for two different messages. In that case you need a CENTRALIZED, ATOMIC seq source, e.g. a PostgreSQL `BIGSERIAL` column (each insert gets an increasing seq assigned by the DB, guaranteed by the transaction) or a Redis `INCR` command (atomic thanks to Redis's single-threaded execution) — both guarantee two concurrent requests never receive duplicate or out-of-order seqs.

