The solution needs the four guards in order, short-circuiting on the first one that fails, and only touches the gateway after all four pass:

```typescript
async createRefund(params: CreateRefundParams): Promise<RefundRecord> {
  // Guard 1 — idempotency: same key returns the original record, no new work.
  const existing = await this.findRefundByIdempotencyKey(params.idempotencyKey)
  if (existing !== null) {
    return existing
  }

  // Guard 2 — intent must be succeeded (nothing captured yet cannot be refunded).
  const intent = await this.findIntentById(params.paymentIntentId)
  if (intent.status !== "succeeded") {
    throw new BadRequestException(
      `Cannot refund intent ${intent.id}: status is ${intent.status}, must be succeeded`,
    )
  }

  // Guard 3 — intent must carry a real gateway charge handle.
  if (intent.chargeId === null) {
    throw new BadRequestException(`Intent ${intent.id} has no gateway charge id — cannot refund`)
  }

  // Guard 4 — requested amount must not exceed what remains refundable.
  const existingRefunds = await this.listRefundsForIntent(intent.id)
  const alreadyRefunded = existingRefunds
    .filter((r) => r.status === "succeeded")
    .reduce((sum, r) => sum + r.amount, 0)
  const remaining = intent.amount - alreadyRefunded
  if (params.amount > remaining) {
    throw new BadRequestException(
      `Refund amount ${params.amount} exceeds remaining refundable amount ${remaining}`,
    )
  }

  // All four guards passed — only now touch the gateway.
  const gatewayResult = await this.callGatewayRefund({
    chargeId: intent.chargeId,
    amount: params.amount,
    idempotencyKey: params.idempotencyKey,
  })

  const record: RefundRecord = {
    id: gatewayResult.refundId,
    paymentIntentId: intent.id,
    amount: params.amount,
    status: gatewayResult.refunded ? "succeeded" : "failed",
    idempotencyKey: params.idempotencyKey,
  }
  await this.saveRefund(record)
  return record
}
```

The key points to preserve: the idempotency guard always runs FIRST and returns immediately — not only to avoid creating a duplicate refund, but to keep a retry of an already-completed request from having to walk through (and possibly fail on) the other business guards again. The `succeeded` and `chargeId` guards are split into two separate errors so the message stays clear and debuggable rather than one vague combined condition. The last guard recomputes `remaining` from all currently-`succeeded` refunds every single time — never trusting a stale cached number unless there's an atomic mechanism to keep it in sync. And most importantly: `callGatewayRefund` — the action that actually moves money — is only invoked after all four guards have passed, never earlier.

