The three requirements correspond to three independent layers of protection, and all three need to be present — skipping any one reintroduces a bug. Full implementation:

```typescript
public async writeChunk(id: string, index: number, buffer: Buffer): Promise<void> {
    const session = this.requireSession(id)

    // (1) Validate bounds BEFORE any I/O — protects the disk from a garbage
    // filename and protects finalize later from reading a nonexistent file.
    if (!Number.isInteger(index) || index < 0 || index >= session.totalChunks) {
        throw new BadRequestException(
            `Chunk index ${index} out of range [0, ${session.totalChunks})`,
        )
    }

    // (2) Overwrite on a FIXED path keyed by index — naturally idempotent:
    // the client can PATCH the same index any number of times and there's
    // still exactly ONE resulting file.
    const path = join(this.cfg.tmpDir, id, `${index}.part`)
    await fs.writeFile(path, buffer)

    // (3) Dedupe at the in-memory layer: only push when the index is NOT
    // already present, then re-sort so GET /status always returns an
    // ascending bitmap.
    if (!session.receivedChunks.includes(index)) {
        session.receivedChunks.push(index)
        session.receivedChunks.sort((a, b) => a - b)
    }
}
```

Step (1) runs before `fs.writeFile` because this is an expected input error (fail-fast) — there's no reason to touch disk for an index that's already known to be wrong. Step (2) chooses `fs.writeFile` (not `fs.appendFile` or an existence check first) precisely for idempotency: the file path is a function of `index`, so PATCHing the same index always produces exactly ONE final result — there's no concept of "second write becomes an error" or "second write becomes a different file." Step (3) is the layer most likely to be skipped: if you just `push(index)` unconditionally, `receivedChunks.length` can grow from a retry even though the number of UNIQUE indices hasn't changed — leading finalize to miscount (this is exactly the bug class seen in the earlier buggy version). Checking `includes(index)` before pushing eliminates that risk entirely, and re-sorting after every addition preserves the "array is always ascending" invariant that `GET /status` depends on.

