The solution hinges on the exact order: check existence → charge (only if none) → record immediately, plus a `failed` handler that only routes to the DLQ on the final failure and keeps `job.data` intact until then.

```typescript
@Processor(PAYMENTS_QUEUE)
export class PaymentsProcessor extends WorkerHost {
    constructor(
        private readonly gateway: PaymentGateway,
        private readonly paymentsRepo: PaymentsRepository,
        private readonly dlqService: DlqService,
    ) {
        super()
    }

    async process(job: Job<ChargeJobData>): Promise<ChargeResult> {
        // 1. Idempotency check FIRST — covers both webhook redelivery
        //    (duplicate job) and retry-after-crash (same job, re-run).
        const existing = await this.paymentsRepo.findByPaymentIntentId(
            job.data.paymentIntentId,
        )
        if (existing) {
            // Already charged successfully before — return the prior
            // result instead of hitting the gateway again.
            return { chargeId: existing.chargeId }
        }

        // 2. Not charged yet — call the gateway exactly once.
        const charge = await this.gateway.charge(
            job.data.paymentIntentId,
            job.data.amount,
        )

        // 3. Record immediately, so a subsequent run (retry or duplicate
        //    job) finds this record and stops at step 1 above.
        await this.paymentsRepo.save({
            paymentIntentId: job.data.paymentIntentId,
            chargeId: charge.id,
            amount: job.data.amount,
        })

        return { chargeId: charge.id }
    }

    @OnWorkerEvent("failed")
    async onFailed(job: Job<ChargeJobData>, error: Error): Promise<void> {
        const max = job.opts.attempts ?? 1
        if (job.attemptsMade >= max) {
            // Final failure — route to a dead-letter queue for a human
            // to inspect/replay instead of silently dropping the job.
            await this.dlqService.push(job.data, error.message)
        }
        // Intermediate failures are expected retries — no DLQ routing yet.
    }
}

// Enqueue side must keep failed job data intact until onFailed runs.
async enqueueCharge(event: StripeWebhookEvent): Promise<{ jobId: string }> {
    const job = await this.paymentsQueue.add(
        "charge",
        { paymentIntentId: event.paymentIntentId, amount: event.amount },
        {
            jobId: event.paymentIntentId, // dedupe redelivered webhooks
            attempts: 3,
            backoff: { type: "exponential", delay: 1000 },
            removeOnComplete: 100,
            removeOnFail: false, // keep data readable for onFailed → DLQ
        },
    )
    return { jobId: String(job.id) }
}
```

The key point isn't "having an idempotency check" per se — it's the ORDER: the check always runs before any side effect that spends real money, and recording "already charged" happens immediately after a successful charge, with no gap where a retry could slip in between. `jobId: event.paymentIntentId` blocks duplication at the enqueue layer (a redelivered webhook doesn't create a new job); the `findByPaymentIntentId` check inside `process()` blocks duplication at the processing layer (even when the exact same job gets retried after a crash). For `onFailed`, the `attemptsMade >= max` guard ensures only a job that has truly "died" (no more retry chances left) gets routed to the DLQ — intermediate failures are normal retry behavior, and routing them too early would flood the DLQ with noise. `removeOnFail: false` is the necessary condition for `job.data` to stay alive until `onFailed` runs — without it, all the DLQ logic above is moot because `job.data` may already have been cleaned up by Redis before the handler gets a chance to read it.

