```typescript
async createOrder(idempotencyKey: string, body: CreateOrderDto): Promise<OrderResult> {
    const key = `idem:${idempotencyKey}`

    // Claim processing rights with SET NX — exactly one request wins.
    const acquired = await this.redis.set(key, "PENDING", "EX", 86400, "NX")

    if (!acquired) {
        const existing = await this.redis.get(key)
        if (existing === "PENDING") {
            // Another request is already processing, no result to return yet.
            throw new ConflictException("Request is still being processed")
        }
        // existing is the JSON result already done — safe to replay.
        return JSON.parse(existing as string)
    }

    try {
        const result = await this.processOrder(body)
        // Overwrite PENDING -> the real result, keeping the same key/TTL.
        await this.redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(result), "EX", 86400)
        return result
    } catch (err) {
        // On failure, release the key so a later retry can try again instead of being stuck at PENDING forever.
        await this.redis.del(key)
        throw err
    }
}
```

The core point: `SET key "PENDING" NX EX ttl` is the single atomic step that decides who gets to process — exactly one request wins, and the losing requests read the current value to know whether to return 409 (`PENDING`) or replay (JSON result). Once `processOrder` finishes, that same key gets OVERWRITTEN with the result — no new key is created — so anyone reading this key afterward sees the real result. The `catch` branch deletes the key on failure so a failed request doesn't leave the key stuck at `PENDING` forever, which would block every legitimate retry after it.

