RRF fuses by RANK, not raw score — which is exactly how it avoids having to normalize two scores in different units.

```typescript
interface RankedDoc {
    docId: string
}

function reciprocalRankFusion(rankedLists: RankedDoc[][], k = 60): Array<{ docId: string; rrfScore: number }> {
    const scores = new Map<string, number>()

    for (const list of rankedLists) {
        list.forEach((doc, index) => {
            const rank = index + 1 // 1-based rank, not 0-based.
            const contribution = 1 / (k + rank)
            scores.set(doc.docId, (scores.get(doc.docId) ?? 0) + contribution)
        })
    }

    return Array.from(scores.entries())
        .map(([docId, rrfScore]) => ({ docId, rrfScore }))
        .sort((a, b) => b.rrfScore - a.rrfScore)
}
```

The core point: for each list, every document gets a contribution of `1/(k+rank)` — `k=60` flattens the gap between adjacent ranks (rank 1 and rank 2 are nearly equal: `1/61` vs `1/62`), so a document that's decent-in-both beats a document that's best-in-only-one. Using a `Map<docId, score>` sums the contribution whenever the same docId appears in multiple lists — this is exactly the "consensus boost" mechanism: a document that shows up well in both BM25 and vector search gets rewarded, since summing two positive contributions is always larger than a single one. Because the unit of fusion is RANK (an integer, not a score in mismatched units), RRF is completely immune to BM25 being unbounded while cosine sits in [0,1] — no score-normalization step is needed at all.

